  "_style": "Write in warm, standard Somali (Af-Soomaali caadi ah, Latin script with standard orthography: double vowels, x, c, q, dh, kh; no regional slang). Always address the user as “adiga” (singular: ku, kaa, -kaaga/-taada, imperatives in singular: “Keydi”, “Dir”); for the couple as a whole use “labadiinaba” / “idinka” and inclusive “-keenna/-teenna” when the app speaks of what belongs to both (“Geedkeenna” is only used as a title the couple says themselves; otherwise “geedkiinna”). The other person is always “lammaanahaaga” (your partner) — never saaxiibkaa/saaxiibtaa, ninkaaga/naagtaada, xaaskaaga; “lammaane” is grammatically masculine, so either use masculine agreement neutrally or, better, rephrase with the plural/impersonal (“la” passive, “labadiinaba”) to avoid marking anyone's gender, and never use wuu/way, isaga/iyada for the partner when a rephrase works. A partner's name placeholder (%@) is never inflected or suffixed (no %@-ow, %@-na, %@-ba, %@-ta): put focus markers and particles as separate words (“%@ ayaa kuu soo diray …”, “Warqad ka timid %@”), avoid wuxuu/waxay after the name since they mark gender, or rephrase. Quotes: curly “ ” outside, ‘ ’ inside; ellipsis “…”; no space before ! ? : ; and keep “ · ”, \\n and every placeholder exactly. Numbers: comma for thousands, point for decimals (1,234.5); dates follow the system locale, in prose day-month-year (14 Nofeembar 2026); 24-hour time. Buttons are short singular imperatives (“Keydi”, “Shaabadee warqadda”, “Isku day mar kale”), no “fadlan” on buttons; titles, tabs, chips and buttons use sentence case (capitalize only the first word and proper names such as Coupley, Premium, AI). Plural categories are [\"one\",\"other\"]: “one” uses the singular noun (“%lld maalin”, “%lld geed”) and “other” the Somali counted form used after numerals (“%lld maalmood”, “%lld geed”, “%lld xusuusood”), written the same way every time. Tone: kind, light and natural like a good Somali app — never cheesy, never word-for-word.",
